## 4 · 📏 SERVER-FIRST — the deploy rule

> ⬜ **Develop and test where you declared. Publish only on an explicit order.**
> ⬜ Flow: declare yours — ⭐ **the engine fixes that publishing needs an order, never where to.**

⚠️ **Why this is project-level and not universal:** it only matters in repos with auto-deploy.
**A repo with auto-deploy publishes on push to its release branch** — declare below which one that is.

> ## ⛔ NEVER NAME A REPO FROM MEMORY
> Declare each one below and read it from there. ⭐ **A repository named from memory is the kind
> of claim that stays wrong for weeks**: a repo can be renamed, moved between organisations, or
> archived, and the old address usually keeps redirecting — so both names resolve and look like
> two live repos. Point at the declaration, and let a command answer which is current.

⬜ **Declare your repos below** — where the workshop lives, where the verified backup goes, where
publishing happens. A repo not declared here is a repo someone will push to by mistake.

| Repo | Role | Remote name on the server |
|---|---|---|
| ⬜ `<forge>/<org>/<workshop-repo>` | ⭐ **the workshop's remote** — where the server pushes daily | `origin` |
| ⬜ `<forge>/<org>/<product-repo>` | **the verified product's backup** (AGPL, GPG-signed by the owner) | `backup` |
| server `~/<workshop-dir>` | the workshop itself — **the source** | — |
| `<site-project>` | the site — **pushing to `main` publishes** | — |

⚠️ **If two remotes are declared, they are not a duplicate by accident.** Verify with
`git remote -v`, not from the table above: the table is a claim, the remote is the fact. Pushing
verified work goes to **all of them** — pushing to only one leaves them divergent, and nothing
announces it.

⛔ **An old repository URL usually keeps redirecting after a move.** Both names resolve and look
like separate repos, so a stale table can cite a dead address and still appear to work.
⭐ **A hand-written repo name is correct exactly once** — re-measure it instead of trusting it.

